  abstract = {The cutoff phenomenon describes a sharp transition in the convergence of a family of ergodic finite Markov chains to equilibrium. Many natural families of chains are believed to exhibit cutoff, and yet establishing this fact is often extremely challenging. An important such family of chains is the random walk on G(n,d)G(n,d)$<$math alttext="\$$\backslash$mathcal\{G\}(n,d)\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mi fontstyle="italic"$>$G$<$/mi$><$mo stretchy="false"$>$($<$/mo$><$mi$>$n$<$/mi$><$mo$>$,$<$/mo$><$mi$>$d$<$/mi$><$mo stretchy="false"$>$)$<$/mo$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$, a random dd$<$math alttext="\$d\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mi$>$d$<$/mi$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$-regular graph on nn$<$math alttext="\$n\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mi$>$n$<$/mi$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$ vertices. It is well known that almost every such graph for d$\geq$3d$\geq$3$<$math alttext="\$d$\backslash$geq 3\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mi$>$d$<$/mi$><$mo$>\geq{}<$/mo$><$mn$>$3$<$/mn$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$ is an expander, and even essentially Ramanujan, implying a mixing time of O(logn)O(logn)$<$math alttext="\$O(\{$\backslash$rm log\} n)\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mi$>$O$<$/mi$><$mo stretchy="false"$>$($<$/mo$><$mi fontstyle="normal"$>$log$<$/mi$><$mi$>$n$<$/mi$><$mo stretchy="false"$>$)$<$/mo$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$. According to a conjecture of Peres, the simple random walk on G(n,d)G(n,d)$<$math alttext="\$$\backslash$mathcal\{G\}(n,d)\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mi fontstyle="italic"$>$G$<$/mi$><$mo stretchy="false"$>$($<$/mo$><$mi$>$n$<$/mi$><$mo$>$,$<$/mo$><$mi$>$d$<$/mi$><$mo stretchy="false"$>$)$<$/mo$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$ for such dd$<$math alttext="\$d\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mi$>$d$<$/mi$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$ should then exhibit cutoff with high probability (whp). As a special case of this, Durrett conjectured that the mixing time of the lazy random walk on a random 33$<$math alttext="\$3\$" overflow="scroll"$><$mrow$><$mn$>$3$<$/mn$><$/mrow$><$/math$>$-re